## **Sub-area: EdTech and e-learning product copy**

### **Genre inventory**

EdTech encompasses both software localization and pedagogical content generation, straddling the line between engineering constraints and educational intent.

| Artifact | Who writes it | Register | Length/Format Norms |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| Gamification UI | Product Team | Enthusiastic, peer-like (chúng mình ↔ bạn / con) | Extremely short. 1-4 words. Emojis and exclamations prevalent. |
| Subtitles & Dubbing | Content Team / Translators | Conversational to Academic | **Strict constraint:** Max \~42 characters per line. Timing and reading speed parameters apply heavily19. |
| Parent Dashboard | EdTech PM | Professional, reassuring (hệ thống ↔ quý phụ huynh) | Short explanatory text, data visualization labels7. |

### **Register and address**

EdTech occupies a precarious dual-register space. For child-facing applications (e.g., VioEdu, Duolingo), the application acts as a peer or a friendly, gamified tutor, frequently using bạn or con depending on the target demographic's age7. When the application communicates with the parent (e.g., progress reports, billing, account linking), it abruptly and necessarily switches to the highly formal quý phụ huynh8.  
Models frequently fail to maintain the segregation between the "Student Portal" and "Parent Portal" registers. This results in jarring semantic drift, such as accidentally addressing a young child with formal financial language (quý khách) or addressing a parent as con during a password reset flow.

